\title{A graph from fourteen points and fourteen blocks}
\author{Human Compression project}
\date{13 October 2026}
\begin{document}\maketitle
\begin{abstract}
We classify the graphs of degree four and girth at least six on
twenty-eight vertices. A short argument forces a bipartition.
The incidence structure on either part then covers all pairs of
fourteen points except a perfect matching. The four blocks through
one point give only three possible local configurations, whose
complete branching certificates have respectively 79, 33 and 42
nodes. Exactly one configuration extends, in exactly one way.
Each edge of the resulting graph belongs to twenty-four six-cycles.
\end{abstract}

\section{Scope and attribution}
All graphs are finite, simple and undirected. The notation
$\operatorname{egr}(n,k,g,\lambda)$ means a connected graph of order
$n$, degree $k$ and girth $g$ in which every edge belongs to precisely
$\lambda$ cycles of length $g$.
Table~2 of Goedgebeur and Jooken~\cite{GJ} gives minimum order
twenty-eight and one isomorphism class for $(k,g,\lambda)=(4,6,24)$.
Its lower bound is attributed to its reference~[8, Theorem~2.3];
the example is attributed to its reference~[15, Example~5.2].
These are respectively Drglin, Filipovski, Jajcay and Raiman,
and Kiss, Miklavi\v{c} and Sz\H{o}nyi. Those priorities are retained.
The present argument recovers the uniqueness entry and identifies
its graph with the stored source example
\nolinkurl{UntilOrder250_161}.

We use the Moore edge tree from our previously reviewed note
\emph{A graph at the girth-six Moore bound}, Lemma~1.
In degree four it has twenty-six distinct vertices: two rooted trees
of depth two, each with level sizes $1,3,9$, joined at their roots.
All internal vertices already have degree four. The leaves form two
sets $A,B$ of size nine, each divided into three branches of size three
according to its parent. An extra edge between tree vertices can
only join $A$ to $B$; any other such edge creates a cycle of length
at most five. This is the precise interface needed below.

\section{Two vertices outside the Moore tree}
\begin{lemma}\label{bipartite}
A graph of degree four and girth at least six cannot have twenty-seven
vertices. If it has twenty-eight vertices, it is bipartite.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Fix a Moore edge tree. A leaf has at most one neighbour in any
opposite branch: two would form a four-cycle through that branch's
parent. A vertex $p$ outside the tree similarly meets at most one
leaf in each branch. Write $a=|N(p)\cap A|$ and $b=|N(p)\cap B|$.
If $x\in N(p)\cap A$ and $y\in N(p)\cap B$, then $y$ meets no
leaf in the branch containing $x$. Indeed, joining $x$ makes a
triangle, while joining another leaf $x'$ in that branch makes
the five-cycle $p,x,\operatorname{parent}(x),x',y,p$.
Consequently each $B$-neighbour of $p$ has at most $3-a$
neighbours in $A$, and each $A$-neighbour has at most $3-b$
neighbours in $B$.

At order twenty-seven, $p$ is the only vertex outside the tree, so
$a+b=4$ and $a,b\leq3$. Every neighbour of $p$ is a leaf with
exactly two neighbours in the opposite leaf set, since its other
neighbours are its parent and $p$. Thus $a,b>0$ force both
$a\leq1$ and $b\leq1$, a contradiction.

At order twenty-eight, let the two outside vertices be $p,q$.
Suppose first that they are nonadjacent. Then $a+b=4$.
Each neighbour of $p$ has at least one neighbour in the opposite
leaf set, since only $q$ can supply another outside neighbour.
The bounds just proved exclude $a=3$ and $b=3$, leaving $a=b=2$.
They now force each of the four neighbours of $p$ to have exactly
one neighbour in the opposite leaf set, and hence also to meet $q$.
Two common neighbours of $p,q$ already give a four-cycle.
Therefore $p,q$ are adjacent.

Now $a+b=3$. No leaf meets both $p$ and $q$, since that would
give a triangle. Hence every neighbour of $p$ has exactly two
neighbours in the opposite leaf set. The same bounds exclude the
mixed possibilities $(a,b)=(1,2),(2,1)$: all three neighbours of
$p$ lie in one leaf set. The same statement holds for $q$.
They must use opposite sets. To see this, every leaf initially
needs three further neighbours besides its parent, so the sums of
the remaining degrees for edges from $A$ to $B$ are $27-t_A$
and $27-t_B$, where $t_A,t_B$ count incidences with $p,q$.
Equality of these sums forces $t_A=t_B=3$.
Put $p$ opposite its three leaf neighbours and $q$ opposite its
three leaf neighbours. This extends the tree's bipartition to
every edge of the graph.
\end{proof}

\section{An incidence problem on fourteen points}
\begin{lemma}\label{cover}
Graphs of degree four and girth at least six on twenty-eight vertices
are precisely the incidence graphs of the following structures:
fourteen points, a perfect matching of noncollinear pairs, and
fourteen blocks of size four covering every other point-pair exactly
once. Each such graph is connected and every edge is on twenty-four
six-cycles.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{bipartite} and regularity the two parts each have
fourteen vertices. Regard them as points and blocks. From any point,
its four blocks lead to twelve distinct other points, since a
repetition would create a four-cycle. Thus exactly one other point
is noncollinear with it. Noncollinearity is symmetric, so these
exceptions form a perfect matching. Every block contains four
different matching classes and covers six allowed point-pairs.
There are $\binom{14}{2}-7=84$ allowed pairs, each covered once.

Conversely, a cover of those pairs by four-subsets has fourteen
blocks. Each point occurs in four blocks, because its twelve allowed
pairs are covered three at a time. The incidence graph is regular
of degree four, is bipartite and has no four-cycle. It is connected:
nonpartner points share a block, and two partners have a third point
collinear with each. Every block meets the connected point set.

For an incident point-block pair $x,L$, choose $y\in L\setminus\{x\}$.
There are ten points collinear with both $x$ and $y$, since precisely
$x,y$ and their two distinct partners are forbidden. Two of these
ten lie in $L$, leaving eight choices of $z$ outside $L$.
The unique blocks joining $x,z$ and $y,z$ are distinct from $L$
and from each other, by uniqueness of the block through $x,y$.
They form a six-cycle through $x,L,y$.
Conversely, a six-cycle containing $xL$, traversed from $x$ to $L$,
determines exactly this pair $y,z$. There are $3\cdot8=24$
choices. In particular the girth is exactly six.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}\label{frames}
Up to relabelling, there are three possibilities for the four
blocks through a specified point.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Label the specified point $0$ and its partner $1$. The four blocks
through $0$ partition the other twelve points into triples.
The six partner pairs among those twelve points each join two
different triples. They therefore give a loopless cubic multigraph
on four vertices, one vertex for each triple.
If its edge multiplicities in positions $01,02,03$ are $a,b,c$,
the four degree equations force the opposite multiplicities in
positions $23,13,12$ to equal $a,b,c$, respectively, and
$a+b+c=3$.
Permuting the four vertices induces every permutation of the
three pairs of opposite edges. Thus the unordered possibilities
are $(3,0,0),(2,1,0),(1,1,1)$.
Conversely each gives a frame: put the two points of each partner
pair in the two indicated triples. Permuting partner pairs and
swapping their points gives the allowed relabellings, so no further
choice of frame remains.
\end{proof}

\section{The complete finite step}
For each frame label all partners as
$\{0,1\},\{2,3\},\ldots,\{12,13\}$. The four fixed blocks cover
twenty-four allowed pairs; sixty remain. Every additional block
is a four-subset of $\{1,\ldots,13\}$ meeting four different partner
pairs and using none of the already covered pairs. These conditions
describe the entire candidate list.

For an uncovered pair $e$, branch on every candidate block containing
$e$ whose six pairs are all still uncovered. Delete those six pairs
and repeat. A branch with no eligible block fails; an empty remainder
is a complete cover. Each step reduces the remainder by six, so the
search terminates. Every cover contains exactly one block through
$e$ and follows exactly one branch. Induction on the number of
remaining pairs proves both completeness and absence of duplicate
labelled covers. Choosing a pair with few eligible blocks affects
only the size of the search.

The supplied certificate records every node, its uncovered pairs,
the chosen pair and every child. Its separate validator recomputes
the full eligible list at each node and checks the exact change of
remainder. It rejects missing branches, reused nodes, extraneous
nodes and incorrectly reported solutions. The complete counts are
\[
\begin{array}{c|r|r}
 (a,b,c)&\text{nodes}&\text{complete covers}\\\hline
 (3,0,0)&79&0\\
 (2,1,0)&33&0\\
 (1,1,1)&42&1
\end{array}
\]
The fourteen blocks of the sole cover, the complete trees and a
literal adjacency-preserving bijection to the source graph are
included in the certificate. The checker also verifies every
six-cycle count directly in both graphs. Graph isomorphism is used
only to propose that bijection; its verification is a direct check
of all vertex pairs.

\begin{theorem}
There is exactly one graph, up to isomorphism, of degree four and
girth at least six on twenty-eight vertices. Its edges each belong
to twenty-four six-cycles. Thus it gives the unique graph at the
minimum order for $(k,g,\lambda)=(4,6,24)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Lemmas~\ref{cover} and~\ref{frames} reduce every graph in question
to one of the three complete searches. Their single resulting
cover gives both existence and uniqueness; Lemma~\ref{cover}
gives the edge count. For minimality, the acquired Moore bound
excludes orders below twenty-six. At order twenty-six the acquired
classification gives the plane of order three, with twenty-seven
six-cycles through each edge. Lemma~\ref{bipartite} excludes
twenty-seven. Alternatively, the source's already reviewed lower
bound gives twenty-eight directly.
\end{proof}

The Moore bound, incidence interpretation and exact-cover method
are established ideas. We assert no new historical classification.
The contribution here is this explicit derivation, its reduction to
three local configurations and a complete small certificate for
the uniqueness assertion in the source.

